<blockquote style="margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px solid
#ccc;padding-left:1ex">
<p>Dear Alex,</p>
<p>Thank you for Kestrel's mark-up of clause 12, which we have discussed
with our purchasing and risk colleagues. Our revised draft (v2) is
attached. In short:</p>
<p>1. General cap: we cannot go down to 100%. As a compromise we offer 150%
of the Annual Fees.<br>
2. Data breach: this must remain uncapped. Kestrel's monitoring service
will connect to our plant networks, and under our group policy every
supplier with network access accepts unlimited liability for data breaches.
We are not able to deviate from this.<br>
3. Indirect loss: we accept a mutual exclusion of indirect and
consequential loss. However, costs of production stoppage caused by
defective products are direct loss for us and must remain recoverable
within the cap.</p>
<p>Please let us know whether Kestrel can accept this. We would like to
finalise clause 12 this week.</p>
<p>As requested, we have added your reference {{REF:G02}} to the subject
line.</p>
<p>Best regards</p>
<p>Dr Felix Brandt<br>
Legal Counsel<br>
Acme Automotive GmbH | Legal Department<br>
T +49 711 0815 3340<br>
f.brandt@acme-automotive.de</p>
<p>Acme Automotive GmbH | Registered office: Stuttgart | Commercial
register: Stuttgart Local Court, HRB 000000 | Managing Directors: Dr Ingrid
Vossberg, Peter Lindqvist</p>
<div>Am 16.10.2026 um 11:40 schrieb Alex Morgan
&lt;alex.morgan@kestrel-eng.co.uk&gt;:</div>
<blockquote style="margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px solid
#ccc;padding-left:1ex">
<p>Dear Julia, dear Felix,</p>
<p>Thank you for the draft Master Supply Agreement. Ahead of our full
comments next week, I attach Kestrel's mark-up of clause 12 (liability),
because it is the point most likely to need discussion. The changes are
tracked and each one has a comment explaining it. In summary:</p>
<p>1. General cap: 100% of the Annual Fees, instead of 200%. This reflects
the value of the supply and market practice for equipment of this kind.<br>
2. Data breach: Kestrel cannot accept unlimited liability. We propose a
separate cap of two times the Annual Fees for data breach and breaches of
clause 10, in addition to the general cap.<br>
3. Indirect loss: we have replaced Kestrel's liability for indirect and
consequential loss, including loss of production, with a mutual exclusion.
Unlimited exposure to production stoppage costs is not insurable for an
equipment supplier.</p>
<p>The carve-outs in clause 12.3 (injury to life, body or health; intent
and gross negligence; product liability; guarantees) stay as they are.</p>
<p>We are happy to discuss this on a call. Tom will suggest some times.</p>
<p>Kind regards<br>
Alex</p>
<p>Alex Morgan<br>
Senior Legal Counsel<br>
Kestrel Engineering Ltd<br>
T +44 114 496 0230<br>
alex.morgan@kestrel-eng.co.uk</p>
<p>This email may contain confidential and legally privileged information.
If you have received it in error, please tell the sender and delete it.</p>
<div>On Thu, 15 Oct 2026 at 07:47, Julia Schmidt
&lt;j.schmidt@acme-automotive.de&gt; wrote:</div>
<blockquote style="margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px solid
#ccc;padding-left:1ex">
<p>Dear Tom,</p>
<p>As discussed, please find attached the first draft of our Master Supply
Agreement for the supply of process and cooling-water pumps, spare parts
and your remote condition monitoring service.</p>
<p>The draft is based on Acme's standard terms for strategic suppliers.
Please note in particular clause 10 (data protection and information
security), because your monitoring service will connect to our plant
networks, and clause 12 (liability).</p>
<p>I have copied Dr Felix Brandt from our legal department, who will handle
the legal points, and your legal team.</p>
<p>We would appreciate Kestrel's comments by Friday 23 October, so that we
can aim for signature in November.</p>
<p>Best regards</p>
<p>Julia Schmidt<br>
